On Nov 26, 1:48 pm, Doug Freyburger <dfrey...@yahoo.com> wrote:
> Janis Papanagnou wrote:
>
> I name gems like "hard green lev 4"

Is there any real point to classifying and carrying
a bunch of gems around that early in the game?

I've been leaving them where they are until at
least mid-game, by which time I am stronger and
can carry more and have a blessed bag of holding
and usually also a reliable way of officially identifying
things, not to mention teleport control, which makes
collecting them MUCH easier.  I have to run back
through all the levels anyway to pick up the magic
items (scrolls and potions and whatnot) that I left
all over the place because I couldn't bear their
weight in the early game[1], so grabbing the gems
while I'm at it at that point is very little extra trouble.

What is the reasoning behind carrying them
around sooner (other than the one hard gem
for engraving mentioned below)?

> I like to carry a hard gem with me as an engraver

Now that makes sense.
I may have to think about doing that.

But still, you only need one for that, right?

> I'll toss soft gems to unicorns

I can seldom get unicorns to hold still long
enough to throw anything at them, especially
in the early game.  (My unicorn horns almost
always come from pet kills.)

> might or might not bother getting exact
> identities after I know all 9 glass types.

I eventually identify them all collaterally,
merely by holding a handful of them in my
inventory each time I happen to be reading
a blessed scroll of identify for some other
reason.  In those kinds of quantities they
weigh almost nothing (and you only need to
hold one of each type, once each), so they
don't significantly decrease the amount of
other stuff you can hold, so there's no real
downside to doing this.

I don't make special trips just for gems,
but if I'm at a stash when I read a blessed
scroll of identify then I grab whichever
gems are there (along with any other
unidentified stuff, and wands and tools
whose charge count I'd like to know)
before reading it, and if I occasionally
decide to burn a scroll "in the field" to
ID something important, I still grab any
unidentified stuff out of my bag of holding
before reading it, and that includes any
gems I had picked up since the last stash.
In this way all gem types are identified
sooner or later.

If I can *cast* identify, then it goes even
faster, because I'm less stingy with that
than with scrolls -- if my power is above
about half and I'm not in the middle of a
combat situation, I go ahead and identify
whatever I just picked up, rather than
waiting until I have a whole bunch of
stuff that needs identified.

